From 24e188eec7e38ca13dae7910971faca322b332c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jackwang0009 <93422958+jackwang0009@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 11:30:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] [upstream-candidate] Write shading objects back

CPDF_PageContentGenerator::ProcessPageObject() had no case for shading
objects, so everything painted with the `sh` operator disappeared as
soon as its content stream was regenerated.

Write them as `q <clip> <ExtGState> <matrix> cm /Name sh Q`. The object
already holds the matrix that was in effect, the clip path and the
general graphics state; `sh` uses neither the current color nor the
line parameters.

The parser now records the resource name the `sh` operator used. The
generator writes that name when the holder's /Shading resources still
map it to the same shading, any other name that does otherwise, and
realizes the shading as a new FXS resource when it is not a resource of
the holder at all (for instance when the object was moved to another
page). A shading dictionary that is a direct object of another
resources dictionary becomes an indirect object in that case, the same
way inline streams do for images, so that both dictionaries refer to
the one shading.

Tests: CPDFPageContentGeneratorTest.ProcessShading (clip, nested cm,
direct and indirect shading dictionaries; generate -> parse -> generate
is stable) and ProcessShadingOnAnotherPage. pdfium_unittests and
pdfium_embeddertests: no new failures.

Verification (ops_diff.py): missing shading events 11 -> 0 on the
synthetic corpus; no-diff pages 55/67 -> 61/67 synthetic (shading-axial,
shading-functions, shading-mesh, shading-radial,
shading-stitching-background, text-clip-modes), 384/388 -> 384/388
public.

Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
